---
title: Shakshuka
course: main
cuisine: middle-eastern
servings: 4
time_active: 15
time_total: 40
leftovers: poor
tags: [weeknight, one-pot]
description: Eggs poached in smoky, harissa-spiked tomato sauce with plenty of feta.
---

Heat @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #large skillet{} over medium. Sauté @onion{1}(diced) and @red bell pepper{1}(diced) until softened, ~{5%minutes}.

Add @garlic{3%clove}(minced) and cook until fragrant, ~{1%minutes}.

Stir in @cumin{2%tsp}, @smoked paprika{2%tsp}, @harissa{1%tbsp}, and @red pepper flakes{1/2%tsp}. Let the spices bloom, ~{30%seconds}.

Pour in @fire-roasted tomatoes{1%can}(28 oz, or crushed tomatoes), season with @salt and @black pepper{}, and simmer until the sauce thickens slightly, ~{12%minutes}.

Scatter half of the @feta{6%oz}(crumbled) over the sauce. Make small wells and crack @eggs{6} into them. Cover, reduce heat to medium-low, and cook until the whites are set but the yolks are still runny, ~{6-8%minutes}.

Top with the rest of the feta, @parsley{1/4%cup}(chopped, or cilantro), and a drizzle of olive oil. Serve straight from the skillet with @crusty bread{1}(loaf, or pita) for dipping.

> A pinch of sugar balances acidic tomatoes. Two 14.5 oz cans work if that's what you have; halve everything for two people. A dollop of Greek yogurt on top is good too.
